      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "On the component of configurations with exactly $n$ points, $N_B(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)=\\sum_{j=1}^n\\mathbf1_B(x_j)$ is Borel for every Borel $B\\subseteq C$. The count of $C$ identifies the component $n$, so every count map is Borel on the full disjoint union. Thus the count-generated sigma-algebra is contained in the sorted-list Borel sigma-algebra.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Let $(T,\\mathcal T)$ be any measurable space. For $H\\in\\mathcal B(C)\\otimes\\mathcal T$, define $M_H(\\eta,t)=\\sum_{x\\in\\eta}\\mathbf1_H(x,t)$. The class of such $H$ for which $M_H$ is measurable with respect to the count sigma-algebra times $\\mathcal T$ contains every rectangle $A\\times D$, since $M_{A\\times D}(\\eta,t)=N_A(\\eta)\\mathbf1_D(t)$. It is closed under relative complements because $M_{(C\\times T)\\setminus H}=N_C-M_H$, and under disjoint countable unions by pointwise convergence of finite partial sums. Rectangles form a generating pi-system, so [F2] proves measurability for every product-measurable $H$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Fix a component with $n$ distinct points and an index $k\\leq n$. Recover the coordinates of the $k$th lexicographic point recursively. Initially the selected prefix is empty and its rank is $r=k$. At coordinate $j$, with measurable prefix $p=(a_1,\\ldots,a_{j-1})$, use step 2.1 on the Borel set $H_j=\\{(x,p,t):x_\\ell=p_\\ell\\ (\\ell<j),\\ x_j<t\\}$, where $t\\in[0,1]$. For each dyadic interval $I=[u,v)$ in coordinate $j$, the number of points matching the prefix and having $x_j\\in I$ is $M_{H_j}(\\eta,p,v)-M_{H_j}(\\eta,p,u)$, hence is measurable. The interval containing the $r$th such coordinate is the first whose cumulative count reaches $r$; the nested interval closures have diameters tending to zero, so their left endpoints converge to that coordinate $a_j$. The count of matching points with $x_j<a_j$ is $M_{H_j}(\\eta,p,a_j)$, measurable by step 2.1 after composition with the measurable prefix and $a_j$. Subtracting it from $r$ gives the positive rank among points with the extended prefix.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "The recursive choice in step 3.1 is the usual lexicographic ordering rule: among points with a fixed prefix, sort by the next coordinate, retain the value at the current rank, then subtract the points strictly below it to obtain the rank among ties. After coordinate $d$, distinctness leaves exactly one point, namely $x_k$. Thus all coordinate functions of every sorted point are count-measurable. The Borel sigma-algebra on the sorted-list component is generated by its coordinate maps, so it is contained in the count-generated sigma-algebra there.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.1",
            "F1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "The count $N_C$ partitions the full configuration space into its countable components. Applying step 4.1 on each component shows that every sorted-list Borel set is count-measurable; step 1.1 proves the reverse inclusion. The displayed sigma-algebra equality follows.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "F1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
          "case": "empty",
          "status": "checked",
          "evidence": "The statement includes the n=0 component; its code is the all-zero padded sequence, while coordinate recovery is needed only for n≥1."
        },
