      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Fix one unit cell $C_i$ and a finite Borel partition $B_0,\\ldots,B_s$ of it. Put $v_j=|B_j|$, so $\\sum_jv_j=1$. Conditional on the cell count $N=n$, the $n$ independent uniform points give category-count probability $n!\\prod_jv_j^{n_j}/\\prod_jn_j!$ for $\\sum_jn_j=n$. Multiplying by the Poisson count probability $e^{-\\lambda}\\lambda^n/n!$ gives $e^{-\\lambda}\\prod_j(\\lambda v_j)^{n_j}/n_j!=\\prod_je^{-\\lambda v_j}(\\lambda v_j)^{n_j}/n_j!$, by [F3]. This factors into the Poisson probabilities of the disjoint cell pieces, including the complement $B_0$; hence their counts are independent Poisson variables with respective means $\\lambda v_j$. Zero-volume pieces have count zero almost surely by the same formula.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Each bounded $A_j$ intersects only finitely many unit cells. Apply step 1.1 to the partition formed by the sets $A_j\\cap C_i$ and their complement in each such cell. Independence within each cell and independence between cell configurations show that all component counts, indexed by both $j$ and $i$, are independent Poisson variables.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For fixed $j$, sum the component counts over its finitely many cells. The sum of independent Poisson variables of means $\\mu_1,\\ldots,\\mu_q$ has mass at $n$ equal to $e^{-\\sum\\mu_i}(\\sum\\mu_i)^n/n!$: expand $(\\sum_i\\mu_i)^n$ by [F2], then use the product of the component masses. Thus $N_{A_j}$ is Poisson with mean $\\lambda\\sum_i|A_j\\cap C_i|=\\lambda|A_j|$. The groups of components for different $j$ are disjoint independent families, so their sums remain independent.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "step 2.1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Let $A_1,\\ldots,A_r$ be pairwise disjoint and, for each $j$, consider a finite count-cylinder event in the restriction to $A_j$. Refine the finitely many counting sets in each event into their disjoint atoms inside $A_j$. Step 3.1 gives independent Poisson counts on all these atoms across every $j$; the event for each restricted configuration is a measurable function only of its own atom counts. Thus finite cylinder events factor across $j$. The restriction sigma-fields are generated by these cylinders, so repeated pi-lambda extension proves independence of the restrictions themselves.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "step 3.1",
            "F4"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "For a finite family of bounded Borel sets $A_1,\\ldots,A_s$, partition their union into the finitely many disjoint Boolean atoms formed by the sets and their complements. Every $N_{A_j}$ is a sum of the counts on these atoms. Translation preserves each atom's volume, so step 3.1 gives the same joint distribution of this whole count vector under $Q_\\lambda$ and its translate. Thus the laws agree on every finite count cylinder. These cylinders generate the configuration sigma-algebra by [F4], and pi-lambda uniqueness identifies the laws. The one-set case gives $E_{Q_\\lambda}N_A=\\lambda|A|$, which is the asserted intensity measure.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "step 3.1",
            "F4"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
        {
