      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For $P\\ll Q$ with density $h$, the negative part of $h\\log h$ is integrable because $-u\\log u\\leq1/e$ for $0\\leq u\\leq1$; the density definition is therefore unambiguous. Also $u\\log u\\geq u-1$ for $u\\geq0$, so $D(P\\Vert Q)=\\int h\\log h\\,dQ\\geq\\int(h-1)\\,dQ=0$. If $P\\not\\ll Q$, the defined value is $+\\infty\\geq0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For bounded $\\phi$, let $Q_\\phi$ have density $e^\\phi/Q(e^\\phi)$ relative to $Q$. When $P\\ll Q$ and $D(P\\Vert Q)<\\infty$, the density logarithm identity gives $D(P\\Vert Q_\\phi)=D(P\\Vert Q)-P(\\phi)+\\log Q(e^\\phi)\\geq0$ by step 1.1. Thus every variational test is at most $D(P\\Vert Q)$; the same upper bound is immediate if $D=+\\infty$. If $P\\not\\ll Q$, choose a measurable $Q$-null set $N$ with $P(N)>0$ and use $\\phi=t\\mathbf1_N$: then $Q(e^\\phi)=1$ and $P(\\phi)=tP(N)\\to\\infty$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "F1",
            "F5",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "In the remaining case $P\\ll Q$, let $h=dP/dQ$ and set $\\phi_m=\\max(-m,\\min(m,\\log h))$, taking $\\phi_m=-m$ on $\\{h=0\\}$. Then $e^{\\phi_m}\\to h$ and $e^{\\phi_m}\\leq1+h$, so [F4] gives $Q(e^{\\phi_m})\\to1$. The negative part of $h\\phi_m$ is bounded by $1/e$, and its positive part increases to $(h\\log h)^+$; hence [F4] gives $P(\\phi_m)=\\int h\\phi_m\\,dQ\\to D(P\\Vert Q)$, including when the positive entropy part is infinite. Combining this with step 2.1 proves the variational identity.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "If $T$ is measurable, every bounded test $\\psi$ on the target pulls back to the bounded test $\\psi\\circ T$ on the source, and $Q(e^{\\psi\\circ T})=(Q\\circ T^{-1})(e^\\psi)$ while $P(\\psi\\circ T)=(P\\circ T^{-1})(\\psi)$. Taking suprema in step 3.1 proves the restriction inequality.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 3.1",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-2",
          "claim": "For a conditioning observation $R$, applying the variational identity pointwise to the conditional density $k_R(r,y)$ shows $$G(R)=\\sup_{\\phi\\in B_b}\\left(E_P\\phi(R,Y)-E_P\\!\\left[\\log\\int e^{\\phi(R,y)}Q_Y(dy)\\right]\\right),$$ where the tests range over bounded measurable functions of $(R,Y)$. The upper bound follows from step 2.1 for each conditional law; the reverse bound follows by using the jointly measurable bounded truncations of $\\log k_R$ from step 3.1 and then [F3]–[F4]. When $R$ is a measurable function of $S$, every test depending on $(R,Y)$ is an admissible test depending on $(S,Y)$ with the same objective value, since the $Q_Y$ normalizer still depends only on $R$. Thus $G(R)\\leq G(S)$; nonnegativity is step 1.1 applied conditionally.",
          "step": "4.2",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "Suppose $P_{XY}$ has density $h(x,y)$ relative to $Q_X\\otimes Q_Y$. Put $h_X(x)=\\int h(x,y)Q_Y(dy)$. By [F3], $h_X$ is a density for $P_X$. Where $0<h_X(x)<\\infty$, [F2] supplies the conditional density $k(x,y)=h(x,y)/h_X(x)$ relative to $Q_Y$; elsewhere use its supplied filler. On the full $P_X$-measure set of finite positive $h_X$, $h=h_Xk$ and $\\log h=\\log h_X+\\log k$ $P_{XY}$-almost everywhere. Integrating this identity, with nonnegative conditional entropies and finite total entropy, gives the displayed chain rule. If a restriction entropy is infinite, step 4.1 makes the joint entropy infinite as well, so no subtraction of two infinite terms is used.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 4.1",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "Steps 1.1–1.3 prove nonnegativity and the variational identity, step 4.1 proves restriction monotonicity, step 5.1 proves the product chain rule, and step 4.2 proves monotonicity under additional conditioning.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "step 5.1",
            "step 4.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "routine_steps": [],
      "boundaries": [
