          "consumer_use": "Facts & Assumptions labels F4; item steps 6.1, 7.1, 8.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral",
          "carrier": "items/thm-monotone-convergence-for-the-integral.md",
          "status": "Read-only published mathematics carrier inspected; this is an interface record, not independent acceptance.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $0\\le f_1\\le f_2\\le\\cdots$ be measurable and suppose $f_n(x)\\uparrow f(x)$\nfor every $x$. Then\n$$\\int f_n\\,d\\mu\\uparrow\\int f\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier statement and proof interface inspected; independent review status remains whatever its carrier records.",
          "consumer_use": "Facts & Assumptions labels F5; item steps 4.1, 5.1, 7.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-fatou-lemma",
          "carrier": "items/thm-fatou-lemma.md",
          "status": "Read-only published mathematics carrier inspected; this is an interface record, not independent acceptance.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(f_n)$ be nonnegative measurable functions. Then\n$$\\int \\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}f_n\\,d\\mu\\le\\liminf_{n\\to\\infty}\\int f_n\\,d\\mu.$$",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier statement and proof interface inspected; independent review status remains whatever its carrier records.",
          "consumer_use": "Facts & Assumptions labels F5; item steps 4.1, 5.1, 7.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-dynkin-pi-lambda",
          "carrier": "items/thm-dynkin-pi-lambda.md",
          "status": "Read-only published mathematics carrier inspected; this is an interface record, not independent acceptance.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $\\mathcal P$ be a pi-system on $X$. Then\n$\\lambda_X(\\mathcal P)=\\sigma_X(\\mathcal P)$. Consequently, if $\\mathcal D$ is\nany lambda-system on $X$ with $\\mathcal P\\subseteq\\mathcal D$, then\n$\\sigma_X(\\mathcal P)\\subseteq\\mathcal D$.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier statement and proof interface inspected; independent review status remains whatever its carrier records.",
          "consumer_use": "Facts & Assumptions labels F6; item steps 5.1."
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Let $A\\subset F_+$ be finite. By [F1], $D(P_{A\\cup C}\\Vert Q_{A\\cup C})$ is finite, so the joint density $h_{A,C}$ relative to $Q_A\\otimes Q_C$ exists. The marginal density $h_A$ is its $Q_C$ integral. On $0<h_A<\\infty$, [F2] gives the conditional density $k_A=h_{A,C}/h_A$ relative to $Q_C$; the exceptional fibers have $P_A$-measure zero. The chain rule in [F2] gives $g(A)=E_P D(P_{C|A}\\Vert Q_C)=D(P_{A\\cup C}\\Vert Q_{A\\cup C})-D(P_A\\Vert Q_A)$, so $g(A)$ is finite and nonnegative.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "If $A\\subseteq B\\subset F_+$ are finite, apply the conditional-increment monotonicity in [F2] to observation of the cell configuration on $A$ and then on $B$. It yields $g(A)\\leq g(B)$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "To identify the supremum, let $\\Lambda_N=[0,N)^d$, the union of $N^d$ unit cells, and order those cells lexicographically. Repeated use of the conditional chain rule decomposes $D(P_{\\Lambda_N}\\Vert Q_{\\Lambda_N})$ as the sum of $g(B_i)$ over its cells $i$, where $B_i$ is the finite set of earlier cells in $\\Lambda_N$. Each $B_i$ is a finite subset of $F_+$ after translating cell $i$ to $C$, so $g(B_i)\\leq\\sup_Ag(A)$ by stationarity and step 2.1. Hence $D(P_{\\Lambda_N}\\Vert Q_{\\Lambda_N})/N^d\\leq\\sup_Ag(A)$. Conversely, fix finite $A\\subset F_+$. For every cell $i$ in the inner cube whose distance from the boundary exceeds the largest coordinate magnitude in $A$, the translated set $i+A$ lies in $B_i$. By step 2.1 and stationarity, $g(B_i)\\geq g(A)$. The proportion of these interior cells tends to one, and all other increments are nonnegative by step 1.1. Taking $N\\to\\infty$ and applying [F1] gives $\\gamma\\geq g(A)$. Taking the supremum over $A$ proves $\\sup_Ag(A)=\\gamma$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Choose an increasing sequence of finite pasts $A_m$ exhausting $F_+$ and set $R=P_{F_+}\\otimes Q_C$. Lift each $k_{A_m}$ to the common space $(\\Omega_{F_+}\\times E_C,R)$. Consistency of the conditional densities shows $E_R[k_{A_{m+1}}\\mid\\mathcal G_m]=k_{A_m}$, where $\\mathcal G_m$ is generated by $A_m$ and $C$; also $E_Rk_{A_m}=1$. By step 3.1, $E_R[k_{A_m}\\log k_{A_m}]=g(A_m)\\leq\\gamma$. For $K>1$, splitting at $k_{A_m}=1$ and using $-u\\log u\\leq1/e$ gives $$\\int_{\\{k_{A_m}>K\\}}k_{A_m}\\,dR\\leq\\frac{\\gamma+1/e}{\\log K}.$$ Thus these martingale densities are uniformly integrable. By [F3] they converge almost surely and in $L^1(R)$ to $k_\\infty\\geq0$ with $E_Rk_\\infty=1$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "For any finite-past cylinder event and any event in $C$, integrate $k_\\infty$ over their product. $L^1(R)$ convergence makes this equal the limit of the same integral against $k_{A_m}$, which is the corresponding joint probability under $P$. These rectangles form a generating pi-system, so [F6] identifies $k_\\infty R$ with $P_{F_+\\cup C}$. Therefore $k_A=E_R[k_\\infty\\mid\\mathcal G_A]$ for every finite $A\\subset F_+$. Fatou applied to $u\\log u+1/e\\geq0$ gives $D(P_{F_+\\cup C}\\Vert P_{F_+}\\otimes Q_C)\\leq\\liminf_m g(A_m)=\\gamma$. Conversely, its variational supremum contains all finite-past tests, so step 3.1 gives the reverse inequality. Hence $E_P\\log k_\\infty=\\gamma$.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "The $L^1(R)$ convergence of $k_{A_m}$ implies convergence in $R$-probability. Since the full joint law has density $k_\\infty$ relative to $R$, absolute continuity transfers this to convergence in $P$-probability. Also $k_\\infty>0$ $P$-almost surely, so continuity of $\\log$ on $(0,\\infty)$ gives $\\log k_{A_m}\\to\\log k_\\infty$ in $P$-probability. For $t\\geq1$, $$E_P\\!\\left[(-\\log k_A)^+\\mathbf1_{\\{(-\\log k_A)^+>t\\}}\\right]=\\int_{\\{0<k_A<e^{-t}\\}}k_A(-\\log k_A)\\,dR\\leq te^{-t}.$$ Thus the negative parts are uniformly integrable; [F4] gives their $L^1(P)$ convergence.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F4",
            "step 4.1",
            "step 5.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-7-1",
          "claim": "Since $E_P\\log k_{A_m}=g(A_m)\\to\\gamma=E_P\\log k_\\infty$ and the negative parts converge in $L^1$, the expectations of the positive parts converge as well. These positive parts converge in probability. For nonnegative variables $Y_m\\to Y$ in probability with $E Y_m\\to E Y<\\infty$, bounded truncations satisfy $E(Y_m\\wedge K)\\to E(Y\\wedge K)$: for any $\\eta>0$ the difference is at most $\\eta+K\\,P(|Y_m-Y|>\\eta)$. Consequently $E(Y_m-K)^+\\to E(Y-K)^+$; choosing $K$ large and then bounding $Y_m\\mathbf1_{Y_m>2K}\\leq2(Y_m-K)^+$ proves uniform integrability, including the finite initial segment. Applying [F4] gives $L^1$ convergence of the positive parts. Together with the negative parts this proves $\\log k_{A_m}\\to\\log k_\\infty$ in $L^1(P)$.",
          "step": "7.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 6.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-8-1",
          "claim": "For any finite $B\\supseteq A_m$, the tower property gives $k_B=E_R[k_\\infty\\mid\\mathcal G_B]$ and $k_{A_m}=E_R[k_\\infty\\mid\\mathcal G_m]$. Conditional expectation is an $L^1$ contraction, so $$\\sup_{B\\supseteq A_m}\\|k_B-k_\\infty\\|_{L^1(R)}\\leq2\\|k_{A_m}-k_\\infty\\|_{L^1(R)}\\longrightarrow0.$$ Absolute continuity of $P=k_\\infty R$ transfers this uniform probability convergence to $P$. The negative-part tail bound in step 6.1 is uniform over all finite $B$, while $g(A_m)\\leq g(B)\\leq\\gamma$ by steps 2.1–1.3. The truncation argument in step 7.1 therefore applies to every sequence $B_m\\supseteq A_m$ with $m\\to\\infty$; if the asserted directed $L^1$ convergence were not uniform, choosing a contradicting sequence $B_m$ would violate that argument. This proves the directed form.",
          "step": "8.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 5.1",
            "step 6.1",
            "step 7.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
