      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Let $W=a+[0,m)^d$ be any integer cube, with $a\\in\\mathbb Z^d$ and integer $m\\geq1$. Translation invariance and [F1] give $D(P_W\\Vert Q_W)\\leq c|W|$. If $A$ is any bounded Borel set, choose one such cube $W$ containing it; by restriction monotonicity [F2], every $P\\in\\mathcal H_c$ satisfies $D(P_A\\Vert Q_A)\\leq C_A:=c|W|$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "For any probability density $h$ relative to a probability $Q$ with $D(hQ\\Vert Q)\\leq C$, and any $K>1$, $$\\int_{\\{h>K\\}}h\\,dQ\\leq\\frac{C+1/e}{\\log K}.$$ Indeed, $-u\\log u\\leq1/e$ on $[0,1]$, while $h\\log h\\geq h\\log K$ on $\\{h>K\\}$. Split the entropy integral at $h=1$ and combine these bounds. Thus, for each fixed cube $W$, the densities $dP_W/dQ_W$ with $P\\in\\mathcal H_c$ have $L^1(Q_W)$ norm one and are uniformly integrable.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Fix $W$. By [F3], the weak closure $K_W$ of these densities in real $L^1(Q_W)$ is weakly compact. It is Hausdorff by [F6]. Every $h\\in K_W$ remains nonnegative and has integral one: testing against each indicator and against $1$ gives weakly closed conditions by [F6]. Also $D(hQ_W\\Vert Q_W)\\leq c|W|$, because [F2] expresses $D$ as the supremum of weakly continuous affine functions of $h$, with continuity given by [F6]. Hence all entropy sublevels used below are closed in their local weak topologies.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F6",
            "step 1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Index all integer cubes $W=a+[0,m)^d$ with $a\\in\\mathbb Z^d$ and integer $m\\geq1$ by their countable set of pairs $(a,m)$, and form the product $K_*:=\\prod_WK_W$. It is compact by [A2] and Hausdorff because each weak $L^1$ topology is Hausdorff by [F6]. Impose the following closed conditions on its tuples: whenever $W\\subseteq V$, the $W$-marginal of $h_VQ_V$ equals $h_WQ_W$; and, for every real translation $x$ and bounded local measurable test $f$, the two marginals containing the supports of $f$ and $f\\circ T_x$ satisfy $E_Pf=E_P(f\\circ T_x)$. Each equality is closed because it tests weak convergence against bounded measurable functions on a containing cube, which are continuous by [F6]. The intersection $K_{\\rm stat}$ of these closed conditions is compact.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A2",
            "F6",
            "step 2.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "A tuple in $K_{\\rm stat}$ defines consistent finite-dimensional laws on the countable standard-Borel cell spaces: for a finite set of cells, choose any integer cube containing it and take that marginal. If two cubes contain the same finite set, a larger integer cube contains both; the imposed nested consistency makes their induced laws agree. By [F4] there is a unique law $P$ on $\\Omega$ with those marginals. The imposed equalities make $P$ stationary, and each origin-anchored cube $[0,m)^d$ retains $D(P_{[0,m)^d}\\Vert Q_{[0,m)^d})\\leq c m^d$. Therefore [F1] gives $I(P)\\leq c$. The extension map identifies $K_{\\rm stat}$ with the set of laws having these local marginals and entropy constraints.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "The same variational inequality controls particle-count tails uniformly. Fix bounded $A$, choose $W\\supseteq A$ as in step 1.1, and let $K\\geq0$, $a>0$, and $M\\in\\mathbb N$. The bounded local test $\\phi_M=a\\min(N_A\\mathbf1_{\\{N_A>K\\}},M)$ in [F2] gives $$aE_P\\min(N_A\\mathbf1_{\\{N_A>K\\}},M)\\leq C_A+\\log E_Q\\exp(\\phi_M).$$ As $M\\to\\infty$, monotone convergence yields $$aE_P[N_A\\mathbf1_{\\{N_A>K\\}}]\\leq C_A+\\log E_Qe^{aN_A\\mathbf1_{\\{N_A>K\\}}}.$$ By [F5] and dominated convergence the logarithmic term tends to zero as $K\\to\\infty$. First choose $a$ large and then $K$ large; this proves uniform integrability of the counts $N_A$ over every law in the closure family, and in particular finite intensity for its limit laws.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "F7",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "The product compactness in step 3.1 first gives compactness for all bounded local tests. For any tame $f$ supported in $A$ with $|f|\\leq b(1+N_A)$, set $f_K=f\\mathbf1_{\\{N_A\\leq K\\}}$. Then $f_K$ is bounded and local, and its expectation is weakly continuous by [F6], while $$\\sup_{P\\in\\mathcal H_c}|E_P(f-f_K)|\\leq b\\sup_{P\\in\\mathcal H_c}E_P[(1+N_A)\\mathbf1_{\\{N_A>K\\}}]\\longrightarrow0$$ by step 5.1. Thus on the entropy level the tame expectation maps are uniform limits of bounded-local continuous maps. Every $P\\in\\mathcal H_c$ supplies its cube densities and gives a tuple in $K_{\\rm stat}$, so the extension map from $K_{\\rm stat}$ is onto; the local expectations just shown make it continuous. Its image $\\mathcal H_c$ is compact in $\\tau_L$.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F6",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "step 5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-7-1",
          "claim": "Each $K_W$ is weakly compact in real $L^1(Q_W)$ and hence weakly sequentially compact by [F3]. Given a sequence in $K_{\\rm stat}$, successively take subsequences converging in the first, second, and later cube coordinates and diagonalize; AC supplies the countable choices. The limit tuple preserves all closed consistency, stationarity, and entropy constraints from step 3.1, so it gives a law in $\\mathcal H_c$ by step 4.1. Count-tail uniformity from step 5.1 upgrades coordinate convergence on bounded local tests to convergence for every tame test by the same truncation estimate in step 6.1. Hence $\\mathcal H_c$ is sequentially compact in $\\tau_L$.",
          "step": "7.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "A2",
            "F3",
            "step 3.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "step 5.1",
            "step 6.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
