          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume AC and let $P\\in\\mathcal P^1_{\\rm stat}(\\Omega)$, with this class defined in [[def-tdmath-local-tame-topology]], be stationary for the translations of [[def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference]]. For a bounded Borel set $G$, write $P_G$ and $Q_G$ for",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier statement and proof interface inspected; independent review status remains whatever its carrier records.",
          "consumer_use": "Facts & Assumptions labels F1; item steps 1.1, 2.1, 5.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-tdmath-poisson-reference-count-properties",
          "carrier": "items/thm-tdmath-poisson-reference-count-properties.md",
          "status": "Current draft carrier inspected; its declared proof state is not promoted by its label.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume AC, fix $d\\geq1$ and $\\lambda>0$, and let $Q_\\lambda$ be the configuration law of [[def-tdmath-poisson-configuration-reference]]. For pairwise disjoint bounded Borel sets $A_1,\\ldots,A_r\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$, the restrictions $\\omega|_{A_1},\\ldots,\\omeg",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier statement and proof interface inspected; independent review status remains whatever its carrier records.",
          "consumer_use": "Facts & Assumptions labels F3; item steps 1.1, 5.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "lem-tdmath-relative-entropy-variational-and-chain-rules",
          "carrier": "items/lem-tdmath-relative-entropy-variational-and-chain-rules.md",
          "status": "Current draft carrier inspected; its declared proof state is not promoted by its label.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Assume AC. Let $P,Q$ be probability measures on one measurable space and use $D(P\\Vert Q)$ as defined in [[def-tdmath-finite-region-relative-entropy]]. Then\n$$D(P\\Vert Q)=\\sup_{\\phi\\in B_b}\\left(P(\\phi)-\\log Q(e^\\phi)\\right),$$\nwhere $B_b$ is the set of bounde",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier statement and proof interface inspected; independent review status remains whatever its carrier records.",
          "consumer_use": "Facts & Assumptions labels F3; item steps 1.1, 5.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-holder-inequality-for-integrals",
          "carrier": "items/thm-holder-inequality-for-integrals.md",
          "status": "Read-only published mathematics carrier inspected; this is an interface record, not independent acceptance.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $(X,\\mathcal A,\\mu)$ be a measure space, let $p,q\\in[1,\\infty]$ be\nconjugate exponents, and let $f,g$ be measurable real-valued functions.",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier statement and proof interface inspected; independent review status remains whatever its carrier records.",
          "consumer_use": "Facts & Assumptions labels F5; item steps 4.1."
        },
        {
          "supplier": "thm-dominated-convergence",
          "carrier": "items/thm-dominated-convergence.md",
          "status": "Read-only published mathematics carrier inspected; this is an interface record, not independent acceptance.",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "exact_statement": "Let $f$ and $(f_n)$ be measurable complex-valued functions such that\n$f_n\\to f$ almost everywhere and $|f_n|\\le g$ almost everywhere for a single\nnonnegative measurable function $g$ with $\\int g\\,d\\mu<+\\infty$. Then\n$f\\in L^1(\\mu)$,\n$$\\int|f_n-f|\\,d\\mu\\longrig",
          "supplier_proof_status": "Supplier statement and proof interface inspected; independent review status remains whatever its carrier records.",
          "consumer_use": "Facts & Assumptions labels F5; item steps 4.1."
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Let $S_N=\\{0,\\ldots,N-1\\}^d$ be the cell indices in $\\Lambda_N$. For $i\\in S_N$, set $A_{i,N}=\\{j-i:j\\in S_N,\\ j<_{\\rm lex}i\\}$; this is a finite subset of the past of the reference cell. Repeated conditional-density factorization from [F3] gives, $P$-almost surely, $$\\log h_N(\\omega)=\\sum_{i\\in S_N}\\log k_{A_{i,N}}(T_{-i}\\omega).$$ Every finite factor is defined $P$-almost surely because the corresponding local relative entropy is finite by [F1].",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Fix $\\varepsilon>0$. By the directed convergence in [F2], choose a finite past $A_*$ such that every finite $B\\supseteq A_*$ satisfies $E_P|\\log k_B-Z|<\\varepsilon$. Put $R=\\max\\{|a_j|:a\\in A_*,1\\leq j\\leq d\\}$, with $R=0$ if $A_*=\\varnothing$. Let $S_N^\\circ$ consist of cells at least $R$ cells from each face of $S_N$. If $i\\in S_N^\\circ$, then $A_*\\subseteq A_{i,N}$, so by stationarity $E_P|\\log k_{A_{i,N}}(T_{-i}\\omega)-Z(T_{-i}\\omega)|<\\varepsilon$. The boundary has $N^d-(N-2R)^d=o(N^d)$ cells (for $N>2R$). For every finite past $B$, $E_P\\log k_B=g(B)\\leq\\gamma$ and $E_P(-\\log k_B)^+\\leq1/e$, hence $E_P|\\log k_B|\\leq\\gamma+2/e$. For $Z=\\log k_\\infty$, $E_P(-\\log k_\\infty)^+=\\int_{0<k_\\infty<1}k_\\infty(-\\log k_\\infty)\\,dR_0\\leq1/e$ on its reference probability $R_0=P_{F_+}\\otimes Q_C$, so [F2] gives $E_P|Z|\\leq\\gamma+2/e$ as well. Summing the interior errors and bounding each boundary error by $2(\\gamma+2/e)$ yields $$\\left\\|N^{-d}\\log h_N-N^{-d}\\sum_{i\\in S_N}Z(T_{-i}\\cdot)\\right\\|_{L^1(P)}\\longrightarrow0,$$ since $\\varepsilon$ was arbitrary.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For $j=1,\\ldots,d$, the shift $U_jf=f\\circ T_{-e_j}$ is a linear isometry on complex $L^2(P)$ by stationarity. Apply [F4] to $N^{-1}\\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}U_j^n$, obtaining strong convergence to the orthogonal projection $P_j$ onto the $U_j$-invariant subspace. The coordinate shifts commute, so every average for coordinate $j$ commutes with every average for coordinate $k$; taking strong limits shows that the $P_j$ commute with each other and with the other coordinate averages. Since all these operators have norm at most one, a finite telescoping estimate gives strong convergence of the product of the $d$ averages to $P_1\\cdots P_d$. This product is the orthogonal projection onto the intersection of the coordinate-invariant subspaces, because the $P_j$ are commuting orthogonal projections. The product averages are exactly the rectangular cube averages $N^{-d}\\sum_{i\\in S_N}Z_M(T_{-i}\\cdot)$, so these converge in $L^2$ for bounded $Z_M$ to a function invariant under every integer shift.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Since $Z\\in L^1(P)$, dominated convergence gives $\\|Z-Z_M\\|_1\\to0$. Cube averages are $L^1$ contractions, so the averages of $Z$ are Cauchy in $L^1$: for large $M$ their distance from the convergent averages of $Z_M$ is at most $\\|Z-Z_M\\|_1$. Let their $L^1$ limit be $s$. Every cube average has mean $E_PZ=\\gamma$, hence $E_Ps=\\gamma$. For each coordinate shift $e_j$, the averaging index cube and its translate have symmetric difference of exactly $2N^{d-1}$ indices, so $\\|U_j A_NZ-A_NZ\\|_1\\leq2\\|Z\\|_1/N$. Passing to the $L^1$ limit gives $U_js=s$. These generators imply invariance under every integer translation.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "step 3.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "Fix $r\\in\\mathbb R^d$, put $G=\\Lambda_N$, $G'=G+r$, and $B=G\\cap G'$. For $N>2\\|r\\|_\\infty$, $B$ contains a cube $J$ of side $N-2\\|r\\|_\\infty$. By [F1] and restriction monotonicity, $D(P_G\\Vert Q_G)\\leq\\gamma N^d$ and $D(P_B\\Vert Q_B)\\geq D(P_J\\Vert Q_J)$; the latter divided by $N^d$ tends to $\\gamma$. Therefore $N^{-d}(D(P_G\\Vert Q_G)-D(P_B\\Vert Q_B))\\to0$. The conditional chain rule [F3] identifies $\\log h_G-\\log h_B$ with a conditional log density. Its mean is this entropy difference, and [F3] bounds its negative-part expectation by $1/e$; thus its $L^1(P)$ norm is at most the entropy difference plus $2/e$. The same bound applies to $G'$. Translation covariance gives $\\log h_{G'}=(\\log h_G)\\circ T_{-r}$; thus the normalized information in $G$ and $G'$ have the same $L^1$ limit. Since $N^{-d}\\log h_G\\to s$, it follows that $s\\circ T_{-r}=s$ $P$-almost surely for this $r$.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "step 4.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "Step 5.1 applies to every fixed $r\\in\\mathbb R^d$. For each rational $q$, the event $\\{s\\leq q\\}$ is therefore invariant modulo null sets under every translation. Full-translation ergodicity makes each such event have probability $0$ or $1$. If $s$ were not almost surely constant, some rational sublevel set would have probability strictly between $0$ and $1$; hence $s$ is constant. Its mean from step 4.1 is $\\gamma$, so $s=\\gamma$. Combining with step 2.1 proves the asserted $L^1(P)$ limit.",
          "step": "6.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 4.1",
            "step 5.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
