          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "In one unit cell, the conditional distribution of point positions given the count is identical for $Q_z$ and $Q_1$. The ratio of their count probabilities at $n$ is $$\\frac{e^{-z}z^n/n!}{e^{-1}/n!}=e^{1-z}z^n.$$ Thus this is the Radon–Nikodym density on that cell.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The cube $\\Lambda_m=[0,m)^d$ is the disjoint union of $m^d$ unit cells. Independence in [F1] makes the finite-cube density the product of the cell densities: $$h_m(\\omega)=e^{(1-z)m^d}z^{N_{\\Lambda_m}(\\omega)}.$$",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The count $N_{\\Lambda_m}$ is Poisson with mean $zm^d$ by [F1]. Directly from its mass function and the exponential series, $$E_PN_{\\Lambda_m}=e^{-zm^d}\\sum_{n\\geq1}n\\frac{(zm^d)^n}{n!}=zm^d e^{-zm^d}\\sum_{k\\geq0}\\frac{(zm^d)^k}{k!}=zm^d.$$ Therefore [F2] gives $$\\frac{D(P_{\\Lambda_m}\\Vert Q_{\\Lambda_m})}{m^d}=\\frac{E_P\\log h_m}{m^d}=1-z+z\\log z$$ for every positive integer $m$.",
          "step": "3.1",
